<%--
Markdown_Viewer

Displays a markdown attachment as the DOCUMENT its author wrote - headings, tables, task
lists, code blocks - rather than as the raw characters of the file. Registered for
`text/markdown` in config('rsx.preview.viewers'), ABOVE the `text/*` entry: the registry is
first-match, and text/* would otherwise take it to Text_Viewer.

WHY RENDERED AND NOT RAW. A markdown file is markup with a rendered form; showing its source
is showing the reader the thing they asked to see, un-run. The raw characters are never far
away - the file downloads and opens as itself - and the preview surface is where the rendered
form belongs. Its sibling Text_Viewer keeps every other text/* mime, where there is no
rendered form and the characters ARE the document.

THE HTML IS SANITISED SERVER-SIDE, TWICE, before it ever reaches this template
(Markdown_Rendition: the parser configured to strip raw HTML and refuse unsafe links, then
HTMLPurifier over the output with an explicit allowlist of exactly the elements GFM emits).
That is why the body interpolates with `<%!= %>` rather than escaping - escaping it here would
print the tags instead of rendering them, and the safety it would add is safety the payload
already carries. Nothing on this side re-sanitises: one sanitiser, at the source.

NO WAITING STATE, and that is a real difference from every other viewer here. There is no
rendition on disk and no background worker: a markdown file is text, the parse happens inside
the request, and the endpoint's answer is always final. The realtime subscription is still
here for the ordinary reasons - the attachment can be replaced or deleted underneath an open
page - and the callback refetches.

States, in the order the template tests them:
  error_data set       the endpoint refused, or the attachment is gone  -> (Preview Unavailable)
  info === null        the first load has not answered yet              -> (Loading...)
  status 'unsupported' not markdown (a registry/mime disagreement)      -> (Preview Unavailable)
  status 'error'       a degraded upload, or the bytes are gone         -> (Preview Unavailable)
  status 'available'
    with empty html    the file is genuinely empty                      -> (This file is empty)
    with html          the rendered document, plus a truncation note when the
                       source was longer than rsx.search.max_text_bytes

Args (the viewer contract Document_Preview passes to every dynamic viewer):
  $attachment_id (required) - the File_Attachment_Model id to read. Throws if absent.
  $file_name     (optional) - shown in no state; accepted so the contract matches its siblings.

Events OUT: 'preview_loaded' {pages:1}. The document scrolls; it does not paginate, so
set_page is a no-op.
--%>
<Define:Markdown_Viewer tag="div">
    <% if (!this.args.attachment_id) { throw new Error('Markdown_Viewer requires $attachment_id'); } %>
    <%
        const _info = this.data.error_data ? { status: 'error', html: null } : this.data.info;
        const _available = _info && _info.status === 'available' && _info.html !== null;
        const _has_html = _available && _info.html !== '';
    %>
    <div class="Markdown_Viewer__frame">
        <% if (!_info) { %>
            <div class="Markdown_Viewer__notice">(Loading...)</div>
        <% } else if (_available && !_has_html) { %>
            <%-- Distinguished from the failure notice below ON PURPOSE: an empty file is a
                 fact about the file, not a fault in the preview, and telling the reader
                 "unavailable" would send them looking for a problem that does not exist. --%>
            <div class="Markdown_Viewer__notice">(This file is empty)</div>
        <% } else if (!_has_html) { %>
            <%-- error / unsupported. Both are the same answer to the reader: there is no
                 document to show. --%>
            <div class="Markdown_Viewer__notice">(Preview Unavailable)</div>
        <% } else { %>
            <div class="Markdown_Viewer__body"><%!= _info.html %></div>
            <% if (_info.truncated) { %>
                <%-- Recorded truncation is not silent truncation: the reader is told that the
                     document continues past what is on screen. --%>
                <div class="Markdown_Viewer__truncated">(This preview shows the beginning of a longer document.)</div>
            <% } %>
        <% } %>
    </div>
</Define:Markdown_Viewer>
